  "response": "<issues>\n- `plt.hist(df[column], bins=10)` raises a `ValueError` when `df` is empty because matplotlib cannot determine the minimum and maximum range for the bins automatically from empty data.\n- `plt.hist` will also raise an error if the specified column contains `NaN` values (missing data), as it cannot plot `NaN`s automatically.\n- Calling `plt.hist` directly adds to the current active figure. If the function is called multiple times, it can result in overlapping plots. Creating a new figure/axes via `plt.subplots()` is a safer approach.\n</issues>\n<solution>\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\ndef task_func(column, data):\n    COLUMNS = [\"Date\", \"Temperature\", \"Humidity\", \"Wind Speed\", \"Precipitation\"]\n    df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=COLUMNS)\n    \n    fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n\n    if df.empty:\n        result = {\n            'sum': 0,\n            'mean': np.nan,\n            'min': np.inf,\n            'max': -np.inf,\n        }\n        # Provide a default range to avoid ValueError when plotting empty data\n        _, _, plot = ax.hist([], bins=10, range=(0, 1))\n    else:\n        col = df[column]\n        result = {\n            'sum': col.sum(),\n            'mean': col.mean(),\n            'min': col.min(),\n            'max': col.max(),\n        }\n        \n        # Drop NaNs before plotting, and handle the case where dropping NaNs leaves empty data\n        plot_data = col.dropna()\n        if plot_data.empty:\n            _, _, plot = ax.hist([], bins=10, range=(0, 1))\n        else:\n            _, _, plot = ax.hist(plot_data, bins=10)\n\n    result['plot'] = plot\n    return result\n</solution>",
